by nicolas-grekas at 2026-10-13T14:07:58Z

Thanks @GromNaN, I squashed the changes below into the commit and rebased after #66639.

`--unbound` is now `--accept-unbound`. The help also says that every key is re-wrapped under the context of the store, never under none.

I kept the wide catch. `DecrypterInterface` allows a `RuntimeException` for an AAD mismatch that the backend can't identify. `KmipKms` does that with PyKMIP, so a narrower catch would break the second run there. Instead, when both attempts fail, the command now reports the failure that isn't the expected `DecryptionFailedException`. A throttled request is then reported as such.

The backend note is in the docblock of `StoredDataKey::wrappingContextFor()`, and in the description for the docs. None of the shipped clients ignores the AAD. Vault refuses a key that isn't derived, and the sealed box and Azure with RSA refuse any AAD. So the risk is a custom client, and the `binding` HMAC stays the check for every backend.
